A||N||26||CoolBlue87GT||Where to keep trailer when not using  -  duh...||||6||
Z||000000||CoolBlue87GT||07-19-2001||07:10 PM||||I've been talking to a few local companies about building a custom auto trailer.<BR>(by custom, I mean size) <P>The U-haul trailer weighed 2000 lbs empty !  They estimate the new one about 1200 lbs.<P>The one roadblock that I couldn't figure out until today, was where to keep it when I wasn't using it.<P>Well, I felt so stupid when I figured it out. Where to keep it ?  - duh -  Under the car in the garage !!<P>The garage is 20' long, and the car is just 14'.  I figure the tongue/hitch assembly would be 'bout 3 - 4 foot long, so that fits !<P>One company suggested using a single axle design.  I'm not so sure about that, as it rules out brakes.<P>I'm leaning towards tandem axle with electric brakes.<P>Anyone have an idea's or suggestion or advice ?<p>[This message has been edited by CoolBlue87GT (edited 07-19-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000001||hugh||07-19-2001||07:28 PM||||Get some references from whomever you want to use,then talk to the trailer owners.You have to be careful when buying a trailer from anyone who is not in the business of just building trailers.If it's not done right,it can be a nightmare.A 2 axle trailer is better,because of braking,balance,and even though heavier,probably easier to pull.There is a solid state(digital) brake controller on the market that is supposed to be better than the older type.||||reg||1
Z||000002||CoolBlue87GT||07-19-2001||07:54 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by hugh:<BR><B> ...There is a solid state(digital) brake controller on the market that is supposed to be better than the older type.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Thanks, Yes, of the few I've spoke with, I can see right through the BS.  The U-haul I used was a nightmear !  It had hydraulic brakes that engaged when slowing, the trailer actually moves forward three inches towards the hitch assembly.<P>It gives an unnerving little bump when first applying the brakes.  Almost feels like you've been bumped from behind.<P>The solid state(digital) brake controller has to be better.  I'll check into it.  Thanks<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000003||Raydar||07-20-2001||02:15 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by CoolBlue87GT:<BR><B> <BR>...It had hydraulic brakes that engaged when slowing, the trailer actually moves forward three inches towards the hitch assembly.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I rented one of those U-Hauls when I went to Tennessee to pick up my "donor" Formula.<BR>Did you ever try backing uphill with that stupid trailer? Can't be done. Locks the brakes. I ended up having to circle my neighborhood, and drive up into the back yard, instead of backing into the driveway.<P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||AusFiero||07-20-2001||03:16 AM||||hmmmmmmmmm An old idea sitting in my head here. I designed a fold away car trailer once. Maybe it could be feasible to build after all. Took up about as much space as box trailer when compacted down. Now all I have to do is find where i put the plans.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.auto-alley.com" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
Z||000005||CoolBlue87GT||07-20-2001||09:25 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B> Did you ever try backing uphill with that stupid trailer?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Oh yes, LOL, I tried to parallel park one on a slight incline.<P>I didn't think about it until now, but that's the reason I was having so much trouble.<P>Gosh, they should tell you these things when you rent them.<P>I used a 99 2 wheel drive Cherokee to pull it. (rated at 5000 lbs)  What did you use ?<P>One of my customers told me that PA has outlawed those type of brakes in that state. He was surprised I didn't get ticketed while in Carlisle.<P>Don't know about that, just know those trailers are to darn heavy. 2000 lbs , and 2700 for the car, thats close to the pulling limit.||||reg||1
Z||000006||CoolBlue87GT||07-20-2001||09:40 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by AusFiero:<BR><B>hmmmmmmmmm An old idea sitting in my head here. I designed a fold away car trailer once. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Sounds interesting.  I'd love to look into an enclosed unit, but the weight would most likely be to much.<P>I did see a home made trailer once that had the front section covered, the cars front end would fit under it. The rest was open.<P>That would at least keep most of the road junk off the car.<P>Good luck with finding that design you made.||||reg||1
Z||000007||skitime||07-20-2001||09:49 AM||||We have a company up here that makes car trailers for the race crowd.  They have light fully enclosed all aluminum trailers.  You don't want anymore stone nicks do you.  Besides you could store it in there all the time so your motorcycle won't fall on it. You also would gain back one garage bay. <P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||CoolBlue87GT||07-20-2001||10:07 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by skitime:<BR><B>We have a company up here .. They have light fully enclosed all aluminum trailers.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sounds good.  The only 'slight' problem I could foresee is I'd have to pull the unit out of the garage, then back the car out to do any work on the car.<P>That would be a pain. Now if it was a stand up height, and had aircondtioning & a recliner, mini fridge, chips, Hmmmm,  That be cool!!<BR>Ha ha  LOL.||||reg||6
Z||000009||AusFiero||07-20-2001||11:13 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by CoolBlue87GT:<BR><B> Sounds good.  The only 'slight' problem I could foresee is I'd have to pull the unit out of the garage, then back the car out to do any work on the car.<P>That would be a pain. Now if it was a stand up height, and had aircondtioning & a recliner, mini fridge, chips, Hmmmm,  That be cool!!<BR>Ha ha  LOL.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Time to sell the tow car CoolBlue and gut a Winnebago for the Fiero to live in  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Leaving the kitchen and a chair of course.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||skitime||07-20-2001||12:32 PM||||The trailer I am describing is standup height and has a door on the drivers side to get out of your car after you drive it in.  Now they also made them with extra room for fridge and recliner but I am sure that it will become to heavy for your Jeep. I am not sure what work needs to be done to a Fiero that has 1700 miles on it but you are probably refering to dusting it off. hehe||||reg||1
